\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, & 1 = 1 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 0 \\cdot 2 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, & -1 = 2 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, & 4 = 2 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186100608 :question/problem "Let $C=AB$. What is the value of $c_{32}$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186100608 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $A$ by the $2$nd column in $B$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{32} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot (-2) \\\\\n&= 4 - 4 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100655 :question/problem "Compute the product\n$\\left\\lbrack \\matrix {-1 \\ 0 \\ -2} \\right\\rbrack \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {2 & -5} \\right\\rbrack$."] [17592186100655 :question/worked-solution "Here, the first factor is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} {\\color{green}\\checkmark} {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} = \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\e